sal exploitation of the Syslog service, followed by deployment of a malicious cron job that installs reverse_ssh, an open-source tool that opens an outbound SSH tunnel from the compromised vCenter server to attacker-controlled infrastructure</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">positive</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_b25dc8d66d7e0b5b" source="e_527eb329cdaeea3a" target="e_6f1bf23c528aecf6"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">installs</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_c8b377c8c0f147f3</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><data key="edge_chunk_id">None</data><data key="edge_json_path">None</data><data key="edge_evidence">The observed attack chain is consistent across victims: path-traversal exploitation of the Syslog service, followed by deployment of a malicious cron job that installs reverse_ssh, an open-source tool that opens an outbound SSH tunnel from the compromised vCenter server to attacker-controlled infrastructure</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">positive</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_5f594669e998b87e" source="e_c2b4a24b66725921" target="e_9fe672f508eac20f"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">opens outbound SSH tunnel from</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_8f53b74342ca502b</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><data key="edge_chunk_id">None</data><data key="edge_json_path">None</data><data key="edge_evidence">The observed attack chain is consistent across victims: path-traversal exploitation of the Syslog service, followed by deployment of a malicious cron job that installs reverse_ssh, an open-source tool that opens an outbound SSH tunnel from the compromised vCenter server to attacker-controlled infrastructure</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">positive</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_09ed78468404436d" source="e_c2b4a24b66725921" target="e_b33ad811f6632a46"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">opens outbound SSH tunnel to</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_367a77fbd649f4c0</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><data key="edge_chunk_id">None</data><data key="edge_json_path">None</data><data key="edge_evidence">The observed attack chain is consistent across victims: path-traversal exploitation of the Syslog service, followed by deployment of a malicious cron job that installs reverse_ssh, an open-source tool that opens an outbound SSH tunnel from the compromised vCenter server to attacker-controlled infrastructure</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">positive</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_1ded27eda3185bd5" source="e_c2b4a24b66725921" target="e_a60c330308d5b05f"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">opens</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_4b64b4cb3de9aa05</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><data key="edge_chunk_id">None</data><data key="edge_json_path">None</data><data key="edge_evidence">The observed attack chain is consistent across victims: path-traversal exploitation of the Syslog service, followed by deployment of a malicious cron job that installs reverse_ssh, an open-source tool that opens an outbound SSH tunnel from the compromised vCenter server to attacker-controlled infrastructure</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">positive</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_057f94d6e20e35c7" source="e_8cd8ce757fc1a249" target="e_a448d4849df2c13e"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">is initiated outbound by</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_0df9b0d5cc15b131</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><data key="edge_chunk_id">None</data><data key="edge_json_path">None</data><data key="edge_evidence">Because the connection is initiated outbound by the victim host, it survives inbound-firewall-only defenses that assume attackers must reach in rather than call out</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">positive</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_81f0da3685e2ad70" source="e_ad16450d4d7b98f8" target="e_2b2f7dd29d81b7cf"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">survives</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_fd78035ec6df7ad5</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><data key="edge_chunk_id">None</data><data key="edge_json_path">None</data><data key="edge_evidence">Because the connection is initiated outbound by the victim host, it survives inbound-firewall-only defenses that assume attackers must reach in rather than call out</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">positive</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_da684b2f91eb6cd8" source="e_8082526e9187e149" target="e_eafc971cf2ca9990"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">reach</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_92b09f87b5ea6a1a</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><data key="edge_chunk_id">None</data><data key="edge_json_path">None</data><data key="edge_evidence">Because the connection is initiated outbound by the victim host, it survives inbound-firewall-only defenses that assume attackers must reach in rather than call out</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">positive</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_38e0d4f38073e9a1" source="e_5a0307ec6adbdba7" target="e_2ffadd82d0071d5c"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">is management plane for</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_c70192f01047de3c</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><data key="edge_chunk_id">None</data><data key="edge_json_path">None</data><data key="edge_evidence">vCenter is the management plane for the hypervisor layer underneath almost every other production system a mid-market organization runs — file servers, ERP application hosts, VPN gateways, backup infrastructure, and increasingly the very SIEM and EDR tooling meant to detect an intrusion like this one</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">positive</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_91d34f347107679d" source="e_3382ebba8a4afd7d" target="e_71a102f80962e355"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">is class For German organizations under</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_1bb69c012f7cc4ea</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><data key="edge_chunk_id">None</data><data key="edge_json_path">None</data><data key="edge_evidence">For German organizations under NIS2, a hypervisor-layer compromise with confirmed unauthorized remote code execution is exactly the class of incident the regulation’s 24-hour early-warning and 72-hour incident-notification obligations were built for — and Germany’s position as the single most-affected country in QUIRSO’s own dataset removes any comfortable assumption that “this is a US problem.”</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">positive</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_19e95db19df6b92d" source="e_3382ebba8a4afd7d" target="e_8637238880a4374c"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">is class of</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_c16139f4468a61b4</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><data key="edge_chunk_id">None</data><data key="edge_json_path">None</data><data key="edge_evidence">For German organizations under NIS2, a hypervisor-layer compromise with confirmed unauthorized remote code execution is exactly the class of incident the regulation’s 24-hour early-warning and 72-hour incident-notification obligations were built for — and Germany’s position as the single most-affected country in QUIRSO’s own dataset removes any comfortable assumption that “this is a US problem.”</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">positive</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_e1b67fc69cbbb75b" source="e_e5fa95b98968f8fc" target="e_7b0d343b348da481"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">were built for For</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_5d3129791b71a7d1</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><data key="edge_chunk_id">None</data><data key="edge_json_path">None</data><data key="edge_evidence">For German organizations under NIS2, a hypervisor-layer compromise with confirmed unauthorized remote code execution is exactly the class of incident the regulation’s 24-hour early-warning and 72-hour incident-notification obligations were built for — and Germany’s position as the single most-affected country in QUIRSO’s own dataset removes any comfortable assumption that “this is a US problem.”</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">positive</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_9a8420c6109f8620" source="e_e0fd0d1c2802e888" target="e_7b0d343b348da481"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">were built for For</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_d4a0bbbc1175a810</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><data key="edge_chunk_id">None</data><data key="edge_json_path">None</data><data key="edge_evidence">For German organizations under NIS2, a hypervisor-layer compromise with confirmed unauthorized remote code execution is exactly the class of incident the regulation’s 24-hour early-warning and 72-hour incident-notification obligations were built for — and Germany’s position as the single most-affected country in QUIRSO’s own dataset removes any comfortable assumption that “this is a US problem.”</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">positive</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_1065963b48a1eb45" source="e_e0212a195724c97f" target="e_6a5b18796f0b7110"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">be applied</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_647972d441b29917</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><data key="edge_chunk_id">None</data><data key="edge_json_path">None</data><data key="edge_evidence">- Mitigate in the interim: if the patch cannot be applied immediately, restrict network access to vCenter’s management interfaces to a hardened jump-host/bastion path only, and block outbound SSH from the vCenter host to anything outside a strict allow-list</data><data key="edge_extraction_method">minie</data><data key="edge_polarity">negative</data><data key="edge_modality">asserted</data></edge><edge id="oe_1059073e48532f79" source="e_a196f386c2b0a3bb" target="e_ddff892c3ca76b5c"><data key="edge_predicate_raw">be described by</data><data key="edge_assertion_id">a_ba51b226e7a000d3</data><data key="edge_source_id">S44</data><data key="edge_source_url">https://diesec.com/2026/08/vmware-vcenter-cve-2026-59310/</data><d